      "title": "Promise Robotics partners with D.R. Horton on physical AI for homebuilding",
      "link": "https://www.therobotreport.com/promise-robotics-partners-d-r-horton-physical-ai-homebuilding/",
      "summary": "Promise Robotics has signed a multi-year partnership with America's largest homebuilder by volume to bring physical AI to construction. The post Promise Robotics partners with D.R. Horton on physical AI for homebuilding appeared first on The Robot Report .",
      "publishedAt": "2026-10-13T13:00:05+00:00",
      "updatedAt": "2026-10-13T13:00:05+00:00",
      "source": "The Robot Report",
      "section_hint": "트렌드",
      "articleText": "Promise Robotics says it can accelerate homebuilding by standardizing component manufacturing. Source: Promise Robotics The construction industry is fragmented and has been relatively conservative about adopting new technologies, but physical AI promises to modernize homebuilding. Today, Promise Robotics said it has signed a multi-year agreement with D.R. Horton, which will use its Factory-as-a-Service capabilities to produce more than 1,250 homes in the U.S. “Homebuilding has long lacked the production infrastructure needed to scale with the speed, predictability, and efficiency of modern manufacturing,” stated Ramtin Attar, co-founder and CEO of Promise Robotics. “Physical AI changes that by creating a new production model that can expand capacity within and across markets. Our goal is to make that capability accessible to builders across North America and support the industry’s long-term growth.” “We started the company on the central thesis that robotics for the homebuilding space needed a very different approach and tech stack,” he told The Robot Report . “We started with mechanical and HVAC, and w’ve built the physical AI infrastructure for building component manufacturing.” Founded in 2021, Promise Robotics said it has integrated robotics, AI , software, and manufacturing systems to connect the homebuilding workflow, from design translation and production planning through automated manufacturing and onsite installation. The Toronto-based company claimed that its Factory-as-a-Service (FaaS) model enables homebuilders to access advanced production infrastructure without the cost, complexity, or risk of developing and operating it themselves. Construction needs to be more repeatable As construction firms navigate persistent housing demand , labor shortages, rising costs, and pressure to increase output, they are searching for more repeatable and scalable ways to expand production, said Promise Robotics. “Unlike traditional manufacturing, especially automotive , where there are five or six big players, the construction industry is large but fragmented,” explained Attar. “The workforce for homebuilding is also highly fragmented between businesses that manage the land, consumer houses, and seasonal trades. It doesn’t invest in the workforce in the same way as traditional manufacturing.” “There’s no such thing as productization, in which the same car is built millions of times with small variations,” he added. “Even in the same home, every floor or wall is different. The clearances, components, and layouts are different.” “To tackle this set of technical and business challenges, we take a distributed manufacturing approach,” said Attar. “Microfactories are a good problem for AI to solve, and we’ve spent years developing the foundational model and systems around construction.” Register now and help us celebrate 20 years of RoboBusiness! Software provides flexibility, scalability, says Promise Robotics Promise Robotics handles that variability and resilience with software that can understand the limits of what’s possible, in terms of motion , building materials, and tools, Attar noted. The company’s software can improve over time, allowing for reduced expenses. It is currently working with its own cloud-based simulations and KUKA industrial robot arms, but it is robot-agnostic, he said. “We have a pretty deep tech stack, incorporating computer vision , LLMs [large language models] for initial prep of data flows, and a reasoning model that’s domain-specific,” said Attar. “For every home we build, we produce 300 new images, 5,000 new tactile sources, and a lot of processes. We’re very data rich, and that’s why we present ourselves as a physical AI infrastructure and not just a technology.” “Our floors are 45 ft. and walls are 20 ft.,” he explained. “The walls have windows and doors in them. We’re also manufacturing stair cages that can be dropped in for fast assembly. Our sweet spot is townhomes between three to four stories. Taller than that, and there’s more need for sprinklers, bracing, and metalwork.” The company can build a two-family home with two stories, a basement, and a garage in one day, not including electronics, window installations, and plumbing. That process typically takes two to three weeks, Attar said. Promise Robotics integrates construction workflows with FaaS Promise Robotics said its technology interprets existing home designs, generates production plans and robotic instructions, orchestrates manufacturing, and connects that production with site installation. Rather than automating isolated tasks, the company said it brings these capabilities together in one integrated environment. “FaaS gives a broad range of homebuilders across geographies access to an integrated production platform,” said Promise Robotics. The FaaS model can help builders improve cycle times, increase capacity, and scale more predictably across communities and markets, it added. “While every house we build is different, the process is the same — handling files, translating them into robotic instructions,” Attar said. “The robots are trained to build with highly variable components. We need to be able to support a lot of different types of builders and different types of products. We’re building everything from single to multiple units to duplexes and fourplexes, townhomes, so forth. And it’s never the same.” Promise Robotics is setting up its initial FaaS facilities in Edmonton and Calgary in Canada and Phoenix. Its own crews operate the systems for a tight feedback loop, Attar said. Promise Robotics’ core technology includes fastening algorithms and can be set to comply with local building codes on things like spacing. “Unlike the harsh environment in Canada, Phoenix required 50% less sheeting and different bracing,” said Attar. “Our system is built to adjust to different markets and generalize through AI.” Promise Robotics is starting its Factory-as-a-Service in three North American cities. Source: Promise Robotics D.R. Horton to help automation scale to U.S. market The company noted that The U.S. market represents the largest opportunity to apply its model at scale. D.R. Horton , America’s largest homebuilder by volume, is the first major U.S. homebuilder to use Promise Robotics’ platform. “At D.R. Horton, we are always looking for innovations that can help us improve how we build. We are continuously focused on improving cycle times, increasing production capacity, and bringing greater consistency and predictability to our operations,” said Brad Conlon, senior vice president for strategic growth and innovation at D.R. Horton. “Promise Robotics ’ integrated platform gives us a new capability to support those goals.” “This is just the tarting point,” said Attar. “We need to earn the trust of each customer and really execute. We value the customers that take a leap with us, but with every home builder, we say, ‘You can buy as much capacity as you like,’ and we provide them with a journey to scale that across many parts of the market.” Promise Robotics is not a vertically integrated developer or a home designer like Reframe Systems , Attar acknowledged. “We’re more about the advancements in robotics, and we’re here to scale when companies need to scale,” he said. About The Author Eugene Demaitre Eugene Demaitre is editorial director of the robotics group at Arrowfly (previously WTWH Media). He was senior editor of The Robot Report from 2019 to 2020 and editorial director of Robotics 24/7 from 2020 to 2023. Prior to working at Arrowfly, Demaitre was an editor at BNA (now part of Bloomberg), Computerworld, TechTarget, and Robotics Business Review. Demaitre has participated in robotics webcasts, podcasts, and conferences worldwide. He has a master's from the George Washington University and lives in the Boston area. Tell Us What You Think! Cancel reply This site uses Akismet to reduce spam. Learn how your comment data is processed. Related Articles Read More > Helm.ai reaches $70M in signed commercial contracts for its foundation models Teradyne invests in Bright Machines to bring robotics to AI infrastructure manufacturing Tackling construction labor shortages: ASI and SoftBank partner on autonomous fleets Raise Robotics to discuss scaling field robots at RoboBusiness",
      "articleUrl": "https://www.therobotreport.com/promise-robotics-partners-d-r-horton-physical-ai-homebuilding/",
      "articleFetchedAt": "2026-10-14T00:00:33.854603+00:00",
      "articleStatus": "fetched",
      "date": "2026-10-14"
