      "title": "Timken Precision Drives puts the motion challenge first in humanoid robots",
      "link": "https://www.therobotreport.com/timken-precision-drives-puts-motion-challenge-first-humanoid-robots/",
      "summary": "Timken combines the expertise of Cone Drive, SPINEA, CGI Motion, and Timken Encoders to develop application-specific motion systems. The post Timken Precision Drives puts the motion challenge first in humanoid robots appeared first on The Robot Report .",
      "publishedAt": "2026-10-14T12:20:19+00:00",
      "updatedAt": "2026-10-14T12:20:19+00:00",
      "source": "The Robot Report",
      "section_hint": "트렌드",
      "articleText": "Timken Precision Drives offers a portfolio of motion technologies for humanoid developers. Source: Timken, Shutterstock What does your humanoid robot’s motion system need to do? For engineers developing humanoids , the answer involves balancing torque, speed, stiffness, weight, positioning accuracy, and available space. Each joint presents its own requirements, and the right solution begins with understanding those demands. That is the approach behind Timken Precision Drives, part of The Timken Co. Bringing together Cone Drive , SPINEA, CGI Motion, and Timken Encoders, the platform combines specialized engineering expertise and complementary technologies to help customers solve their motion control challenges. The conversation starts with the application: What motion is required? What loads must the system support? Where is the current design falling short? And what needs to change to achieve the desired performance? Timken engineers around the application Developers of humanoids face different challenges across load-bearing joints, compact wrists, end effectors , and other moving assemblies. A system that meets the needs of one mechanism may require a different approach elsewhere in the robot. Timken Precision Drives said it works with customers to evaluate those requirements and identify an appropriate path forward. That may involve a standard component, a modified design, or a custom solution that brings multiple technologies together. Understanding the application early allows engineering teams to consider how gearing, motors , bearings, and position feedback will interact within the available space. It also helps guide decisions about reduction ratio, load capacity, allowable deflection, duty cycle, and service life. Register now and help us celebrate 20 years of RoboBusiness! Complementary technologies, custom possibilities Timken said its platform’s breadth gives engineers multiple ways to approach a motion challenge. Cone Drive contributes strain wave gearing expertise for applications requiring compact packaging, high torque density, and zero-backlash positioning. Component and housed configurations provide a foundation for application-specific designs, with opportunities to explore further integration based on customer requirements. SPINEA brings cycloidal gearing and integrated actuation expertise. TwinSpin reducers and DriveSpin servo actuators offer options for applications where torsional stiffness, torque transmission, and load-handling capability are important design considerations. CGI Motion adds precision planetary and micro gearing, along with custom design and manufacturing capabilities for compact mechanisms. That expertise supports the exploration of motion solutions where size, configuration, and performance requirements call for a tailored approach. Timken Encoders complements the mechanical portfolio with magnetic position feedback technologies , including absolute and incremental options. Timken Precision Drives said these combined capabilities enable it to look across technologies as it works with customers to develop solutions around their specific motion requirements. Timken’s platform brings together motion technologies from multiple units. Source: Timken Precision Drives Collaboration from concept through production Custom development begins with a clear understanding of the problem, according to Timken. A customer may need to reduce the size of an assembly, improve positioning accuracy, increase stiffness, or integrate feedback into an existing design. Timken Precision Drives said it brings application engineering and specialized product expertise into those discussions, supported by Timken’s global engineering and manufacturing resources. The goal is to work alongside the customer to determine which combination of technologies and design choices best addresses the application, whether refining an existing mechanism or developing something new. Bring your motion challenge to Timken Precision Drives Timken Precision Drives plans to exhibit at Humanoids Summit Silicon Valley on Dec. 1 and 2 at the San Mateo County Event Center. Visitors to the company ‘s booth can explore its platform’s motion technologies and discuss their design challenges with the team. Bring requirements, integration questions, or the humanoid problem you have yet to solve. Tell Us What You Think! Cancel reply This site uses Akismet to reduce spam. Learn how your comment data is processed. Related Articles Read More > Will ‘boring’ robots win in the real world? Why robotic form should follow workflow Boston Dynamics gives more insight into its redesigned humanoid hand Harmonic Drive to share how integrated actuators can reduce engineering at RoboBusiness FireDome builds autonomous ‘artillery’ for wildfire defense",
