      "title": "Tree Appreciator",
      "link": "https://simonwillison.net/2026/Oct/14/tree-appreciator/",
      "summary": "Tool: Tree Appreciator Another game experiment with Claude. This one used the default Claude iPhone app (chat mode, not Claude Code mode), where I prompted Claude Opus 5.5 Max with: Build a three js game called \"Tree appreciator\" - it shows you two algorithmically generated trees and the game mechanics is about appreciating the differences between them. Think hard about how to make sure it is both fun and beautiful. Somewhat embarrassingly given my recent talk , I've found that telling Opus 5.5 \"make sure it is fun\" works uncomfortably well. In this case Claude crunched for 3 hours and 7 minutes before retuning a cosy little spot-the-difference game with music and animation and achievements that's quite compelling to play. Tags: game-design , javascript , ai , generative-ai , llms , claude , vibe-coding , three-js",
      "publishedAt": "2026-10-14T21:23:01+00:00",
      "updatedAt": "2026-10-14T21:23:01+00:00",
      "source": "Simon Willison",
      "section_hint": "AI",
      "articleText": "Tool: Tree Appreciator Simon Willison’s Weblog Newsletter Sponsored by: Pomerium — Static OAuth scopes won't stop AI agent breakouts. Upgrade to Pomerium's agentic access management trusted by NVIDIA and OpenAI 14th October 2026 Tool Tree Appreciator — Tree Appreciator is a slow spot-the-difference game in which each round grows two 3D trees from one seed and asks the player to find a handful of small differences between them, such as a missing bough, a visiting bird, or an early-turning branch. Players tap a difference in either tree to notice it, can use limited hints and drag or zoom to view the trees from different angles, and earn appreciation points, leaves and herbarium entries for each pair completed. Another game experiment with Claude. This one used the default Claude iPhone app (chat mode, not Claude Code mode), where I prompted Claude Opus 5.5 Max with: Build a three js game called \"Tree appreciator\" - it shows you two algorithmically generated trees and the game mechanics is about appreciating the differences between them. Think hard about how to make sure it is both fun and beautiful. Somewhat embarrassingly given my recent talk , I've found that telling Opus 5.5 \"make sure it is fun\" works uncomfortably well. In this case Claude crunched for 3 hours and 7 minutes before retuning a cosy little spot-the-difference game with music and animation and achievements that's quite compelling to play. Posted 14th October 2026 at 9:23 pm Recent articles Hello Python 3.15, goodbye 3.10, and now I can use 3.11 - 12th October 2026 A new feature for my blog, built using my voice - 9th October 2026 Claude Haiku 5.5 - 7th October 2026 We're going to need default hard budget caps on pretty much everything - 3rd October 2026 This is a beat by Simon Willison, posted on 14th October 2026 . game-design 25 javascript 767 ai 2,283 generative-ai 2,025 llms 1,991 claude 318 vibe-coding 101 three-js 5 Monthly briefing Sponsor me for $10/month and get a 