    {
      "title": "How ADI is helping robotics startups scale",
      "link": "https://www.therobotreport.com/how-adi-is-helping-robotics-startups-scale-2/",
      "summary": "Robotics businesses need help keeping up with innovations as they scale. Analog Devices Inc. or ADI is offering its Emerging Tech Hub. The post How ADI is helping robotics startups scale appeared first on The Robot Report .",
      "publishedAt": "2026-10-15T16:39:38+00:00",
      "updatedAt": "2026-10-15T16:39:38+00:00",
      "source": "The Robot Report",
      "section_hint": "트렌드",
      "articleText": "The ADI cleanroom in Beaverton, Ore. Sponsored by Analog Devices Inc. Robotics companies are developing new capabilities at a rapid pace, from autonomous mobile robots to humanoid systems. However, keeping up with technological advances is one part of the challenge. The most critical thing is to turn a successful prototype into a commercially viable product that can be manufactured and deployed at scale. “The biggest challenge I see robotics startups facing today is the speed at which the industry moves,” says Cameron Villone, Senior Product Technology Manager at Analog Devices Inc. (ADI). In most cases, a startup excels in adopting next-generation technologies in sensing, motion control, and AI, but commercialization requires additional expertise in electronics, system integration, validation, and manufacturing. These demands can often stretch the resources of smaller engineering teams. ADI is developing its Emerging Tech Hub within the Analog Garage to help robotics startups and established companies overcome these challenges. The Emerging Tech Hub is intended to give companies access to ADI engineers, prototyping and testing resources, and manufacturing and sourcing expertise as they prepare their designs for production. The challenges of scaling robotics innovation ADI was recently highlighted in The Race to Make AI Physical whitepaper explaining how physical AI brings together sensing, signal conversion, perception, reasoning, control, computing, and validation. These functions must work together for a robot to perform reliably. Even when a robot makes the right decision, executing that decision depends on accurate sensor data, timely control signals, and sufficient power to drive its actuators. These engineering requirements continue to change as the product progresses from prototype to production. For example, a prototype needs to demonstrate whether technology can work, but commercialization requires the complete system to perform consistently under different operating conditions. ADI describes this through four stages: Idea, Prototype, Validate, and Scale. The initial stages focus on developing and demonstrating the concept, while validation involves testing the integrated system, identifying potential problems, and optimizing the design before production. Scaling introduces additional manufacturing and supply chain requirements. Robotics companies need to secure components, coordinate production, and maintain consistent quality as volumes increase. “ADI’s expansive experience in sensor development and semiconductor manufacturing allows for knowledge-driven support when these robotic developers move from idea to production,” says Villone. These changing demands for a robotics startup are the gaps ADI’s Emerging Tech Hub is intended to address. The Emerging Tech Hub brings expertise and resources The Emerging Tech Hub is a robotics-focused initiative being developed to bring startups and established robotics companies closer to ADI’s technical teams, including engineers implementing newly released or still-in-development technologies. Villone describes the approach as “co-development,” where when a company approaches the Emerging Tech Hub, the first step is to establish their current capabilities, what they are trying to achieve, and how to algin align these goals with the Emerging Tech Hub’s mission. From there, the two sides can determine what kind of collaboration is appropriate. The engineering support from ADI will depend on the robotics company’s stage and needs. For example, a mature robotics developer might need a particular semiconductor component, while another may be trying to solve a different engineering problem, prompting the Emerging Tech Hub to find specialists within ADI. An early-stage startup might need broader guidance to clarify its architecture and identify what further development requires. The Emerging Tech Hub brings three areas of support together. The technical expertise ranges from robotics, control, edge AI, power, and system integration. Development resources include laboratories, prototyping tools, reference designs, testing and validation capabilities, and hands-on engineering support. For companies preparing to increase production, it also provides sourcing guidance and industry connections. Villone said ADI has recruited robotics experts from across the industry to be better equipped to handle fast paced robotic development. He cited ADI’s GMSL™ technology as one example of expertise that can help robotics developers address camera connectivity and integration. This support extends beyond simply component selection to decisions about how technologies fit within the wider robotic system. The Emerging Tech Hub is still being prepared for wider external participation, with applications expected to open by the end of 2026. Bridging startup agility and corporate resources Startups need flexibility to test ideas quickly and retain control of their technology. But working with a large technology company can introduce a tension between speed and structure. Villone acknowledged that some startups are reluctant to work with larger corporations because they worry about what such a relationship could mean for their business. However, he sees an opportunity when the intentions are clear. ADI’s interest is in enabling robotics applications using its semiconductors, while startups can benefit from expertise and resources that would be difficult to develop independently. The partnership works best when both sides understand what they want to accomplish. This is the same model ADI is proposing for its Emerging Tech Hub. The company describes the relationship as collaborative, with flexible, startup-friendly engagement , with the and startups retaining their intellectual property. These principles allow companies to seek technical support without surrendering ownership of their innovations. The same questions will be explored at RoboBusiness on October 20 during “Robotics Startup Boot Camp Part 2: Challenges & Best Practices for Working with Corporate Partners.” Colm Prendergast, senior director and head of the Emerging Tech Hub at ADI, will join representatives from MassRobotics and WiBotic to discuss how startups can pursue proofs of concept and pilots, how corporations evaluate potential partners, and how startup agility can coexist with corporate processes. The discussion aims to make those relationships more practical for companies preparing to commercialize their technologies. Conclusion A robotics company requires engineering expertise, system validation, production planning, reliable component sourcing, and industry partnerships to successfully bring the innovation to market. ADI’s Emerging Tech Hub addresses these challenges by connecting robotics companies with technical expertise and development resources. To learn more about building successful corporate partnerships, attend Robotics Startup Boot Camp Part 2 at RoboBusiness , where industry leaders will discuss the opportunities and challenges of working together. Register now to attend. Register now and help us celebrate 20 years of RoboBusiness! About the author Abhishek Jadhav is a technology writer who brings an engineer’s perspective to cover electrical, electronics, and computing domains. He writes in-depth analysis that connects device-level design with emerging trends in AI hardware, embedded systems, and intelligent automation. His articles have appeared in Informa TechTarget, The Register, EE Times, Embedded.com , Power Electronics News , and other trade publications. Tell Us What You Think! Cancel reply This site uses Akismet to reduce spam. Learn how your comment data is processed. Related Articles Read More > Precision In Motion. Vishay Precision Group, Inc. (VPG) to Showcase Custom Sensing Capabilities for Humanoid Robotics at RoboBusiness 2026 Your Robot’s Safety Functions Already Work. What If the Input Lies? Designing long-travel axes: When rack and pinion outperforms ball screws and linear motors How to select the right rack and pinion system for high-precision linear motion",
